Let $\mathbf{A}$ be an $n \times r$ matrix such that its Gram matrix $\mathbf{A}'\mathbf{A}$ is invertible. Given $u < n$, define the block matrix $\mathbf{B} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ as follows:
$$\mathbf{B} = \begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{I}_u & \mathbf{0} \\
\mathbf{0} & \mathbf{0}
\end{bmatrix},$$
where $\mathbf{I}_u$ is the $u \times u$ identity matrix. Determine whether all the eigenvalues of the matrix $(\mathbf{A}'\mathbf{A})^{-1}(\mathbf{A}'\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A})$ lie within the interval $[-1, 1]$. Provide a proof or counterexample.

Solve the system of congruences using the Chinese Remainder Theorem:

\[\begin{align*}
x &\equiv 3 \pmod{7} \\
2x &\equiv 5 \pmod{13} \\
x^3 &\equiv 1 \pmod{23}
\end{align*}\]
